WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.235
An arithmetic expression with binary operators

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.630
is naturally a binary tree.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.880
Each operand, a variable or a number,

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:10.821
is a leaf.

00:00:10.821 --> 00:00:13.815
Each operator is an internal node

00:00:13.815 --> 00:00:16.980
whose two subtrees are its operands.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:20.909
The operator applied last sits at the root.

00:00:20.993 --> 00:00:23.407
Operators of equal precedence

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.155
group from the left

00:00:25.155 --> 00:00:28.485
so here the last one is the final +

00:00:28.485 --> 00:00:30.316
with right operand H.

00:00:30.316 --> 00:00:33.812
Its left operand is everything before it

00:00:33.812 --> 00:00:37.225
whose root is the -, and so on down.

00:00:37.308 --> 00:00:40.305
Evaluating the tree means evaluating

00:00:40.388 --> 00:00:41.970
the subtrees first.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:45.304
Traversing this tree writes the

00:00:45.393 --> 00:00:48.059
expression in three notations.

00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:52.057
Preorder gives +, -, /, +, A B,

00:00:52.057 --> 00:00:53.301
and so on.

00:00:53.301 --> 00:00:57.121
Inorder gives A + B, /, C * D

00:00:57.121 --> 00:01:00.320
and so on: the ordinary infix form

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.630
but without any brackets.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:06.528
And postorder gives A B +, C D *

00:01:06.528 --> 00:01:08.214
/, and so on.

00:01:08.214 --> 00:01:11.319
This answers the slide's question:

00:01:11.319 --> 00:01:13.448
for an expression tree,

00:01:13.448 --> 00:01:17.262
the postorder is exactly the postfix form,

00:01:17.262 --> 00:01:20.545
also called reverse Polish notation.

00:01:20.633 --> 00:01:22.851
Each operator comes right

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:24.980
after its two operands.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.722
The preorder is the prefix form,

00:01:27.722 --> 00:01:29.217
or Polish notation,

00:01:29.217 --> 00:01:32.208
with each operator before its operands.

00:01:32.282 --> 00:01:34.076
The inorder is the infix

00:01:34.151 --> 00:01:35.796
form without brackets,

00:01:35.871 --> 00:01:38.412
and read with the usual precedence,

00:01:38.487 --> 00:01:40.506
it gives a different value.

00:01:40.506 --> 00:01:43.122
To get a correct infix expression,

00:01:43.122 --> 00:01:46.187
put brackets around every operator node.

00:01:46.262 --> 00:01:48.430
The method infix in the notes

00:01:48.504 --> 00:01:49.850
does exactly that.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:52.327
We normally write infix:

00:01:52.327 --> 00:01:55.488
each operator between its two operands.

00:01:55.488 --> 00:01:57.859
Infix needs precedence rules:

00:01:57.859 --> 00:01:59.361
* before +,

00:01:59.361 --> 00:02:01.811
and brackets to override them.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.656
That makes it awkward for a machine

00:02:04.735 --> 00:02:07.185
to evaluate from left to right.

00:02:07.185 --> 00:02:08.133
In postfix,

00:02:08.133 --> 00:02:11.215
2 + 3 * 4 becomes

00:02:11.215 --> 00:02:13.270
2 3 4 * +.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.202
2 * 3 + 4

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:19.644
becomes 2 3 * 4 +.

00:02:19.644 --> 00:02:22.990
And with brackets,

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:26.433
(2 + 3) * 4 becomes

00:02:26.433 --> 00:02:27.580
2 3 + 4 *.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.914
The precedence has already been worked

00:02:30.993 --> 00:02:33.899
out when the postfix form is written

00:02:33.977 --> 00:02:36.176
so postfix needs no brackets

00:02:36.255 --> 00:02:38.139
and no precedence rules.

00:02:38.139 --> 00:02:40.338
Spaces separate the tokens

00:02:40.338 --> 00:02:42.930
2 3 is two operands.

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:46.664
Postfix is easy to evaluate with a stack

00:02:46.664 --> 00:02:48.126
from lecture 9.

00:02:48.126 --> 00:02:50.896
Read the tokens from left to right.

00:02:50.896 --> 00:02:53.127
An operand is simply pushed.

00:02:53.127 --> 00:02:56.358
An operator pops the right operand first

00:02:56.358 --> 00:02:59.820
then the left operand, applies the operator

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.590
and pushes the result.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:02.762
At the end

00:03:02.762 --> 00:03:05.453
the stack holds exactly one number

00:03:05.453 --> 00:03:07.620
the value of the expression.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.755
Here is the slides' example:

00:03:12.755 --> 00:03:16.286
2 3 2 4 * + *.

00:03:16.286 --> 00:03:20.499
It is the postfix form of

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:23.574
2 * (3 + 2 * 4).

00:03:23.574 --> 00:03:27.787
2 is pushed, then 3, then 2

00:03:27.787 --> 00:03:29.040
then 4.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:32.550
* pops 4, the right operand

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.717
then 2, the left one.

00:03:34.717 --> 00:03:36.974
2 * 4 is 8

00:03:36.974 --> 00:03:38.870
and 8 is pushed.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.627
+ pops 8 and 3.

00:03:41.627 --> 00:03:44.220
3 + 8 is 11.

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:47.461
The last * pops 11 and 2

00:03:47.461 --> 00:03:48.452
22.

00:03:48.452 --> 00:03:51.177
One number is left on the stack

00:03:51.177 --> 00:03:53.490
so the value is 22.

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:55.003
One trap.

00:03:55.003 --> 00:03:57.387
For 8 2 -

00:03:57.387 --> 00:04:00.601
the stack holds 8 and 2

00:04:00.601 --> 00:04:02.363
with 2 on top.

00:04:02.363 --> 00:04:04.954
The first pop gives 2

00:04:04.954 --> 00:04:08.168
and that is the right operand

00:04:08.168 --> 00:04:11.070
because it was pushed last.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:14.796
So the answer is 8 - 2, 6.

00:04:14.885 --> 00:04:17.462
Popping into left first would

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:20.660
compute 2 - 8, -6.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.536
For + and * the order

00:04:23.626 --> 00:04:25.069
does not matter

00:04:25.159 --> 00:04:27.955
so this bug hides until a minus

00:04:28.045 --> 00:04:30.210
or a divide comes along.

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:34.298
The tree itself can be evaluated directly

00:04:34.298 --> 00:04:37.221
and that is a postorder traversal.

00:04:37.221 --> 00:04:39.727
To evaluate an operator node

00:04:39.727 --> 00:04:43.402
first evaluate its left and right subtrees

00:04:43.402 --> 00:04:45.490
then apply the operator.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.998
2 times 4 is evaluated first: 8.

00:04:48.998 --> 00:04:51.587
Then 3 plus 8: 11.

00:04:51.587 --> 00:04:54.510
Then 2 times 11: 22.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.957
The program prints the fully

00:04:57.032 --> 00:04:59.803
bracketed infix form, and 22.

00:04:59.878 --> 00:05:02.049
The stack method does exactly

00:05:02.124 --> 00:05:03.697
the same computation

00:05:03.772 --> 00:05:05.719
it just receives the nodes

00:05:05.794 --> 00:05:07.367
already in postorder.

00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:08.340
Same answer.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:11.555
Now the reverse question.

00:05:11.555 --> 00:05:15.245
Given only the preorder of a tree

00:05:15.245 --> 00:05:17.669
can you draw the tree?

00:05:17.669 --> 00:05:20.304
No: A with left child B

00:05:20.304 --> 00:05:23.045
and A with right child B

00:05:23.045 --> 00:05:25.680
both have preorder A, B.

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:29.211
But given the preorder and the inorder

00:05:29.211 --> 00:05:31.168
the tree is determined

00:05:31.168 --> 00:05:34.430
provided all the elements are distinct.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:36.789
Two facts do all the work.

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:39.261
First, the first element of the

00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:40.961
preorder is the root.

00:05:40.961 --> 00:05:42.816
Second, in the inorder

00:05:42.893 --> 00:05:44.979
everything left of the root

00:05:45.056 --> 00:05:46.601
is the left subtree

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:48.688
and everything right of it

00:05:48.765 --> 00:05:50.387
is the right subtree.

00:05:50.387 --> 00:05:53.787
Once you know the size of the left subtree

00:05:53.787 --> 00:05:56.028
the preorder splits as well

00:05:56.028 --> 00:05:58.500
and you repeat on each subtree.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.560
Here is the example.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:07.138
The inorder is D, C, E, B, A, U, Z, T, X, Y

00:06:07.138 --> 00:06:12.593
and the preorder is

00:06:12.593 --> 00:06:13.337
A, B, C, D, E, X, Z, U, T, Y.

00:06:13.337 --> 00:06:16.809
The preorder starts with A

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:19.040
so A is the root.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:23.413
In the inorder, D, C, E, B are left of A

00:06:23.413 --> 00:06:26.847
and U, Z, T, X, Y are right of it.

00:06:26.847 --> 00:06:30.085
The left subtree has 4 nodes

00:06:30.085 --> 00:06:34.304
so its preorder is the next 4 elements

00:06:34.304 --> 00:06:38.327
B, C, D, E, and the rest, X, Z, U, T, Y

00:06:38.327 --> 00:06:41.270
belongs to the right subtree.

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:46.241
Now the left subtree of A:

00:06:46.241 --> 00:06:48.935
preorder B, C, D, E, and inorder D, C, E, B.

00:06:48.935 --> 00:06:50.432
Its root is B.

00:06:50.432 --> 00:06:54.424
Everything is left of B in the inorder

00:06:54.424 --> 00:06:57.617
so B's left subtree is D, C, E

00:06:57.617 --> 00:07:00.810
and its right subtree is empty.

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:05.730
The left subtree of B has preorder C, D, E

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:08.656
and inorder D, C, E: root C

00:07:08.656 --> 00:07:12.390
with left child D and right child E.

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:17.770
Back to the right subtree of A:

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:22.123
preorder X, Z, U, T, Y, and inorder U, Z, T, X, Y.

00:07:22.123 --> 00:07:23.797
Its root is X.

00:07:23.797 --> 00:07:28.820
U, Z, T are left of X, and Y is right of it

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:31.610
so Y is X's right child.

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:37.232
Last, the left subtree of X:

00:07:37.232 --> 00:07:40.047
preorder Z, U, T, and inorder U, Z, T.

00:07:40.047 --> 00:07:43.682
Root Z, with children U and T.

00:07:43.682 --> 00:07:46.261
Every node is placed

00:07:46.261 --> 00:07:49.310
and the tree is finished.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.906
In Java, the recursive method works on

00:07:52.985 --> 00:07:54.970
a slice of each sequence

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:58.303
n elements of the preorder starting at p

00:07:58.303 --> 00:08:01.875
and n elements of the inorder starting at i

00:08:01.875 --> 00:08:04.017
describe the same subtree.

00:08:04.097 --> 00:08:06.398
The root is the first element

00:08:06.478 --> 00:08:08.224
of the preorder slice.

00:08:08.224 --> 00:08:11.637
A map gives its position r in the inorder

00:08:11.637 --> 00:08:14.970
and the left subtree has r − i nodes.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:18.415
Then two recursive calls build the

00:08:18.506 --> 00:08:21.601
left subtree and the right subtree

00:08:21.692 --> 00:08:23.694
from the right slices

00:08:23.694 --> 00:08:26.880
and makeBT puts the root on top.

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:30.886
Finding the root in the inorder by scanning

00:08:30.971 --> 00:08:33.777
would cost O(n) for every node

00:08:33.777 --> 00:08:36.667
O(n²) for a skewed tree.

00:08:36.752 --> 00:08:39.558
A hash map from value to position

00:08:39.643 --> 00:08:41.938
makes each lookup O(1)

00:08:41.938 --> 00:08:44.659
so the whole rebuild is O(n).

00:08:44.659 --> 00:08:47.975
The map also detects duplicate values

00:08:47.975 --> 00:08:50.270
which this method refuses.

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:51.814
Now predict.

00:08:51.814 --> 00:08:55.107
A tree has preorder

00:08:55.107 --> 00:08:58.401
3, 9, 20, 15, 7, and inorder

00:08:58.401 --> 00:09:00.329
9, 3, 15, 20, 7.

00:09:00.409 --> 00:09:02.658
How many nodes does the left

00:09:02.738 --> 00:09:04.747
subtree of the root have?

00:09:04.747 --> 00:09:07.960
And what does the whole tree look like?

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.530
Pause the video and rebuild it.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:15.816
The root is 3.

00:09:15.816 --> 00:09:19.892
Only 9 is left of 3 in the inorder

00:09:19.892 --> 00:09:23.684
so the left subtree has one node: 9.

00:09:23.684 --> 00:09:27.381
The right subtree has preorder

00:09:27.381 --> 00:09:31.646
20, 15, 7, and inorder 15, 20, 7:

00:09:31.646 --> 00:09:33.542
root 20

00:09:33.542 --> 00:09:36.670
with children 15 and 7.

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:40.686
This is the exercise tree of part 3

00:09:40.686 --> 00:09:44.260
and the program RebuildDemo prints it.

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:47.263
Can a tree also be rebuilt from

00:09:47.349 --> 00:09:49.573
its inorder and postorder?

00:09:49.573 --> 00:09:52.654
Yes, by the same argument mirrored

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:54.708
the last element of the

00:09:54.794 --> 00:09:56.676
postorder is the root

00:09:56.676 --> 00:10:00.441
and the inorder splits around it as before.

00:10:00.527 --> 00:10:04.121
The method fromInPost rebuilt the same

00:10:04.207 --> 00:10:07.715
A … Y tree from its inorder and postorder.

00:10:07.715 --> 00:10:09.170
Both pairs work.

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:12.543
What about preorder and postorder?

00:10:12.543 --> 00:10:14.514
Both tell you the root

00:10:14.596 --> 00:10:16.814
but neither tells you where

00:10:16.896 --> 00:10:18.703
the left subtree ends.

00:10:18.703 --> 00:10:22.399
With preorder A, B, C and postorder C, B, A

00:10:22.399 --> 00:10:25.602
A is the root and B is its only child

00:10:25.684 --> 00:10:28.066
but nothing says whether B is

00:10:28.148 --> 00:10:30.120
a left or a right child

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:32.830
and the same goes for C under B.

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:35.878
So there are four different trees

00:10:35.878 --> 00:10:38.020
not two as the slide says.

00:10:38.099 --> 00:10:41.035
A program in the notes tries all 5

00:10:41.114 --> 00:10:43.891
binary trees with preorder A, B, C

00:10:43.891 --> 00:10:47.382
and exactly these four have postorder

00:10:47.382 --> 00:10:47.882
C, B, A.

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:51.191
In general, every node with exactly one

00:10:51.274 --> 00:10:53.256
child doubles the count

00:10:53.338 --> 00:10:56.064
that is precisely the information

00:10:56.146 --> 00:10:58.459
preorder and postorder lose.

00:10:58.459 --> 00:11:02.010
If the tree is full, they do determine it.

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:05.319
To sum up the lecture's last part.

00:11:05.319 --> 00:11:07.646
1: for an expression tree

00:11:07.646 --> 00:11:10.455
the postorder is the postfix form

00:11:10.455 --> 00:11:12.542
and a stack evaluates it

00:11:12.542 --> 00:11:15.511
pop the right operand, pop the left

00:11:15.511 --> 00:11:16.876
push the result.

00:11:16.876 --> 00:11:19.043
2: preorder and inorder

00:11:19.043 --> 00:11:21.129
or inorder and postorder

00:11:21.129 --> 00:11:24.420
of distinct elements determine the tree

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:27.710
and a hash map makes the rebuild O(n).

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:31.402
3: preorder and postorder do not

00:11:31.492 --> 00:11:34.382
because each node with one child

00:11:34.472 --> 00:11:36.820
could hang on either side.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:37.965
Try this.

00:11:37.965 --> 00:11:41.584
Rebuild the tree with inorder

00:11:41.584 --> 00:11:45.380
4, 2, 5, 1, 6, 3, and postorder

00:11:45.380 --> 00:11:47.851
4, 5, 2, 6, 3, 1.

00:11:47.851 --> 00:11:49.970
Then give its preorder.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:52.988
Next lecture: binary search trees

00:11:52.988 --> 00:11:56.440
where the inorder finally comes out sorted.

